      <h1>What Happens to an Inherited House With Tenants in Texas</h1>
      <p class="hero-sub">A tenant living in the home adds a layer to an already hard situation. Here's what happens to the lease, the rent, and the deposit, and what your options are.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container article-layout">
      <article class="article-body">

        <div class="article-intro">
          <p>When someone passes away owning a rental property, the tenant is still living there and the lease is still in effect. That surprises a lot of families. The house doesn't become empty, and the tenant's rights don't disappear because the owner did. If you've inherited a home with a tenant in it, you have more options than it may feel like, but it helps to understand the basics first.</p>
          <p>We're licensed Texas Realtors, not attorneys. Landlord and tenant rules depend on the lease and the facts, so check with a probate attorney or a landlord-tenant attorney before acting. This guide covers the real estate side.</p>
        </div>

        <h2 id="the-lease">What Happens to the Lease?</h2>
        <p>In general, a lease does not end just because the landlord has died. The lease is a contract tied to the property, and it carries on with the estate and then with whoever ends up owning the home. The tenant can stay through the end of the lease term, and the terms, including the rent amount, generally continue to apply.</p>
        <p>If the tenant is month-to-month, the arrangement continues the same way, though either side can end it by giving proper written notice. The notice period and rules depend on the lease and Texas law, so confirm the details before sending anything.</p>

        <h2 id="rent-and-deposit">Who Collects the Rent and Holds the Deposit?</h2>
        <p>While the estate is open, rent generally belongs to the estate, and it usually falls to the executor or administrator to collect it, keep the property insured, and pay the property's expenses. If you aren't the person with that authority yet, check with the estate's attorney before collecting rent or talking with the tenant about changes.</p>
        <p>The security deposit is the tenant's money, held for them. It has to be accounted for and passed along properly if the property changes hands, so find out where the deposit is and keep good records. Mishandling it is a common source of disputes later.</p>

        <div class="callout-box">
          <strong>Keep the tenant informed.</strong> A tenant who suddenly gets no communication, or who doesn't know where to send rent, may stop paying or leave without notice. A short, kind note explaining who to contact and where to send rent goes a long way and protects the property's income.
        </div>

        <h2 id="options">Your Options</h2>

        <div class="qa-list">
          <div class="qa-item">
            <h4>Keep it as a rental</h4>
            <p>If the tenant is paying and the numbers work, continuing the rental can be a reasonable choice, either for a while or long term. You'd take on being a landlord, or hire a property manager. It's a real commitment, so run the numbers on rent, taxes, insurance, and repairs first.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="qa-item">
            <h4>Sell it with the tenant in place</h4>
            <p>Many homes sell with the tenant still living there, and the buyer takes over the lease. This often appeals to investors and cash buyers, who are comfortable with an occupied property. It can be the simplest route when you don't want to deal with the tenant's move-out, though a tenant-occupied sale usually brings a different price than a vacant one.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="qa-item">
            <h4>Wait for the lease to end, then sell vacant</h4>
            <p>If the lease ends soon, waiting can let you list the home vacant, with easier showings and a wider pool of buyers. The tradeoff is carrying the home through that period, so compare the wait against what it costs you.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="qa-item">
            <h4>Agree to a move-out with the tenant</h4>
            <p>Sometimes a tenant is willing to leave early, especially with some help such as a flexible move-out date or a payment toward moving costs. That has to be a voluntary written agreement. You can't simply force a tenant out, and an improper attempt can create legal trouble, so get an attorney's guidance on how to do this correctly.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="mistakes">Things to Avoid</h2>

        <div class="process-steps">
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">Avoid</div>
            <h4>Changing the locks or entering without proper notice</h4>
            <p>A tenant has rights to the home while the lease is in effect. Entering without proper notice or shutting off utilities can create serious problems for you.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">Avoid</div>
            <h4>Telling the tenant they have to leave</h4>
            <p>The lease generally stays in force. Ending a tenancy requires following the lease and the legal process, not just asking.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">Avoid</div>
            <h4>Spending the security deposit</h4>
            <p>The deposit isn't estate money to use for repairs or bills. Keep it separate and documented.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">Avoid</div>
            <h4>Selling before checking the lease</h4>
            <p>The lease terms affect your price, your timeline, and who can show the home. Read it before you list or accept an offer.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="how-we-help">How We Can Help</h2>
        <p>We work with owners of tenant-occupied and inherited properties. We can give you an honest value for the home as it sits today, show you what selling with the tenant in place compares with selling vacant, and make a direct cash offer if that fits. Your attorney handles the legal side, and we handle the real estate side.</p>

